.TH iron-vault-recipient-remove 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-recipient\-remove \- Remove an enrolled recipient by its `age1...` key. The service shows what will change, then asks for the approval passphrase. It refuses the operational recipient, the last recovery recipient and, in a Docker Compose stack, the `host\-unlock` recipient whose identity the unlock handoff holds. It says that copies of `identity.age` made before still open with the removed key
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault recipient remove\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIRECIPIENT\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Remove an enrolled recipient by its `age1...` key. The service shows what will change, then asks for the approval passphrase. It refuses the operational recipient, the last recovery recipient and, in a Docker Compose stack, the `host\-unlock` recipient whose identity the unlock handoff holds. It says that copies of `identity.age` made before still open with the removed key
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIRECIPIENT\fR>
The recipient\*(Aqs `age1...` key, as `recipient list` prints it
